!Reel - a video player for RISC OS                        0.1.21
===================================================================

Reel plays video files in a desktop window, with sound. It uses FFmpeg
5.1 (the riscos-ffmpeg port, with its NEON code), so it plays almost
anything: MP4, MKV, WebM, AVI, MOV, MPEG; H.264, HEVC, VP8, VP9, AV1,
MPEG-1/2/4, Theora and more. It needs an ARMv7 machine with NEON
(Raspberry Pi 2/3/4/400, Titanium, IGEPv5, ...).

Using it
--------
Double-click !Reel: its icon (a film reel) appears on the icon bar. Then:

  - drop a video file on the icon, or on Reel's window; or
  - double-click a video file in the Filer (while Reel is loaded, or
    at any time for the types ticked in File types, below); or
  - choose Open address... on the icon's menu, for a web address (see
    Web addresses, below).

File types > on the icon bar menu lists the video file types your
machine knows (from MimeMap: AVI, MP4, MKV, MPEG, ...). Tick one and
double-clicking a file of that type starts Reel with it even when Reel
isn't loaded; untick it to give it back. Reel remembers the ticks in
Choices:Reel.Types, which its !Boot runs, so they last after a restart
(another program that also claims the type when its !Boot runs later
wins). While Reel is loaded it plays any video double-clicked, ticked
or not.

MP4, MKV and WebM files often arrive from other computers as Data files
named like film/mp4, since those formats have no settled RISC OS
filetype. While Reel is loaded, double-clicking a Data file whose name
ends in a video extension (/mp4, /mkv, /webm, /avi, /mov, /m4v, /mpg,
/mpeg, /ts, /vob, /wmv, /flv, /ogv, /3gp) plays it. Tick "Data, by name"
in File types for that to work when Reel isn't loaded too: Data files
then go through Reel's RunData, which starts Reel for those names and
passes any other Data file on to whatever ran Data files before.

Drop several files at once (one drag from the Filer) and they play one
after another: a playlist. Hold Shift as you drop to add files to the
playlist instead of starting a new one.

The window shows the picture with a row of controls under it:

  Pause/Play    stop and start (a click on the picture does the same)
  < 10s  10s >  go back or forward 10 seconds
  the bar       click anywhere on it to go there
  the time      where you are / how long the video is; then the speed
                if it isn't normal (e.g. 1.5x), and A or AB while
                A-B repeat is set. "Buffering" when a web address
                can't be read fast enough; "Opening" while it opens
  the volume    the green bar: click along it to set the volume.
                Reel remembers it for next time
  Full          full screen (Escape or F to come back). Full screen,
                the pointer hides when the mouse is left alone for 2
                seconds, and comes back when it moves
                (*Set Reel$NoHidePointer 1 keeps it)

Keys (click in the window first):
  Space         pause / play
  Left, Right   back / forward 10 seconds
  Down, Up      back / forward 1 minute
  F             full screen on and off
  Escape        leave full screen
  M             the mini player on and off
  D             deinterlace: Auto, On, Off in turn
  A             A-B repeat: set A, then B (it repeats), then off
  N, P          the next / previous file in the playlist
  Q             close the video
  I             the media info window on and off
  S             stats on the picture on and off (below)
  V             subtitles hidden / shown again
  J             the next subtitle track (after the last: none)
  #             the next sound track (files with more than one;
                after the last, the first again)
  Page Down, Up the next chapter / back to the start of this one
                (the one before, within 2 s of its start)
  . (full stop) paused: the next picture (playing: pauses first)
  , (comma)     paused: the picture before

Resize the window by dragging the grip in its bottom right corner (the
three diagonal lines), or choose a size from Window size on its menu; the
picture keeps its shape. When a playlist is playing, the title shows
which file it is, e.g. "(2/5)".

Menu on the window:
  Media info          the media info window (below)
  Stats on the picture  the figures YouTube's "Stats for nerds" shows,
                      drawn on the picture's top left corner (below)
  Full screen         the whole screen (Escape to come back)
  Mini player         a small player above the icon bar (below)
  Keep on top         the mini player comes back to the front
  Window size >       Half (50%), Actual size (100%), Double (200%) of
                      the video's size, or Fit the screen (as big as
                      fits above the icon bar). Never narrower than the
                      controls. The grip resizes it to any size
  Picture >           Fit: the whole picture, with black bars
                      Fill (crop): fill the window, cutting off the edges
                      Original size: pixel for pixel, centred
                      Stretch: fill the window, out of shape
  Deinterlace >       Auto: only the pictures the file marks as
                      interlaced (TV recordings, DVDs); On: every
                      picture (for files that don't say); Off: never.
                      Remembered
  Speed >             0.5x to 2x; the sound keeps its pitch
  Sound track >       for files with more than one (e.g. languages)
  Subtitles >         None, or a track: the file's own, and subtitle
                      files (below)
  Chapters >          the file's chapters, with where each starts:
                      choose one to go there
  Playlist >          jump to a file; Clear the rest
  A-B repeat          as the A key
  Loop                play the file (or the playlist) again and again
  Fast decode         cheaper, rougher decoding, for files that are too
                      much for the machine (it skips the deblocking
                      filter, so you may see blocks). Without it, Reel
                      skips the filter by itself when a file decodes
                      too slowly to keep up (a 4K or 4:4:4 file), and
                      puts it back once decoding has time to spare;
                      *Set Reel$NoAutoFast 1 stops that
  Vsync (full screen) full screen waits for the screen's refresh before
                      each picture: no tearing. Untick it to draw at
                      once (a little faster; you may see a tear line).
                      *Set Reel$NoVsync 1 starts with it off
  Hardware acceleration  show the picture through a hardware overlay
                      when the machine has one (below). On by default,
                      remembered; shaded without the VideoOverlay module
  Close               close the video

Menu on the icon: Info (the program's name, author and version), Open
address... (below), Loop, Log, Quit. The video's own details are Media
info, on the window's menu.

Web addresses
-------------
Reel plays from the internet too: http:// and https:// addresses of
video files (MP4, WebM, MKV, ...) and of HLS streams (.m3u8). https uses
RISC OS's AcornSSL module (RISC OS 5.28 or later), which !Run loads.
Give Reel an address by:

  - Open address... on the icon's menu: type it, or paste it with
    Ctrl-V (or the Paste button), then press Return or click Play;
  - dropping a file with addresses in it on the icon or the window: a
    URI or URL file, a text file with one address a line (several make
    a playlist), an M3U playlist, or yt-dlp's output (below).

Reel reads ahead in the background (up to 10 seconds, or 32MB), so a
slow connection doesn't hold up the desktop. While an address opens,
what was playing carries on (with nothing playing, the window opens and
says "Opening"). If the connection can't keep up, the time says
"Buffering" and the picture and sound wait. Seeking works whenever the
server allows it (most do). Media info's Reading line shows how much has
been read ahead, and the Address and Sound from rows say where from.

YouTube and other sites: yt-dlp
-------------------------------
The address of a web page (a YouTube page, say) isn't a video: Reel says
"that's a web page, not a video". yt-dlp (https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp),
run on another computer, finds the video's own address(es), and Reel
plays what it prints. For example (all on one line):

  yt-dlp -g -f "bv*[height<=720][vcodec^=avc1]+ba[ext=m4a]/b[height<=720]"
         "<the page's address>" > clip.txt

-g prints the address to play or, as here, two: the best video and the
best sound, which sites keep apart. Reel plays them together. Or:

  yt-dlp -j -f "bv*[height<=720][vcodec^=avc1]+ba[ext=m4a]/b[height<=720]"
         "<the page's address>" > clip.json

-j (--dump-json) prints everything: Reel uses the video's title, its
addresses, and the HTTP headers the site asks for. For a playlist page
-j gives a line per video, and Reel plays them in turn.

Copy the file to RISC OS (as Text, or JSON) and drop it on Reel. Or copy
yt-dlp's output as text and paste it into Open address: several lines
are played at once.

The -f choice asks for H.264 (avc1) at 720 lines or fewer, which a
Raspberry Pi 4 plays smoothly; VP9 and AV1 play too, but cost more.
YouTube's addresses work only from the internet connection that ran
yt-dlp (a computer on the same home network is fine) and only for a few
hours: after that, run yt-dlp again.

Mini player
-----------
M, or Mini player on the window menu, swaps the window for a small one
with no title bar in the bottom right of the screen, just above the icon
bar: the picture (320 pixels wide on most screens), Play/Pause, the
position bar and Normal. Drag the picture to move it, and the grip in
its bottom right corner to make it bigger or smaller (it keeps the
video's shape); it opens there, at that size, next time. Double-click
the picture, click Normal or press M for the normal window again. The
menu, keys and everything else work as in the window.

The mini player is made to be watched while you do other things:
  - the picture is shrunk properly: a big reduction (a 1280-pixel video
    in a 320-pixel mini player is a quarter of its size) first halves
    the picture, averaging each 2x2 block of pixels, then scales what's
    left, so fine detail stays smooth instead of breaking up into jagged
    edges, for about the same work;
  - it decodes a little less: the deblocking filter is skipped on the
    pictures no other picture is predicted from (about 15% less work for
    typical H.264, and nothing you can see at that size). Going back to
    the normal window turns it off again, with nothing left over. (Fast
    decode on the menu skips it on every picture, in either window.)
Media info's Converting line says when the picture was halved.

Keep on top: RISC OS has no "always on top" for ordinary windows, so
another window can cover the mini player. With Keep on top ticked, the
mini player looks about once a second while playing and, if something
has been opened over it, comes back to the front. It never takes the
keyboard from what you're typing in.

Deinterlacing
-------------
TV recordings, DVDs and camcorder video are often interlaced: each
picture is two half-pictures (fields) taken a moment apart, so anything
moving shows comb-like stripes. Reel removes them with FFmpeg's yadif,
which looks at the pictures before and after to fill in each missing line:
sharp where things are still, smooth where they move. One picture a
frame (25 a second for PAL). On ARM it runs in NEON (added in this port;
it gives exactly the same picture as FFmpeg's plain C). Media info shows
whether the file says it's interlaced (Scan) and, while playing, what
deinterlacing is doing and its cost a picture (Deinterlacing).
With Auto, progressive video (most downloads) costs nothing.

Hardware acceleration
---------------------
With the VideoOverlay module (in !System on RISC OS 5; !Run loads it) and
hardware that has video overlays (the Raspberry Pi does), Reel copies
each picture as it comes from the decoder (YUV, "YV12") into an overlay,
and the display itself scales it to the window and turns it into RGB on
its way to the monitor. Converting, scaling and plotting each picture are
gone: on a Pi 4 that is about a third of the work for a 720p video in a
window, and more full screen, so files that were only just too much for
the machine play smoothly. Decoding is still done by the CPU.
On the Pi the overlay sits on top of everything on the screen, so Reel
hides it, and draws the picture as before, whenever a window or menu is
over the picture, while paused, at the end, and while it asks you
something; it comes back by itself. Screen grabs don't see the overlay.
Media info's Converting and Drawing lines say when it's in use (or why
not). The overlay changes picture at the screen's refresh, so a picture
that comes before the last change has happened waits for it (Drawing:
"waited"), while Reel carries on decoding; if a newer one is due by
then, the newer one is shown instead ("replaced"). At 60 fps on a 60 Hz
screen a few are replaced; at 24-30 fps there should be none.
Media info opens beside the player (or above or below it) where there's
room, so it doesn't cover the picture.
Whenever an overlay isn't possible, the picture is drawn exactly as
before, with no message: the option off, no VideoOverlay module, the
display refusing the size or running short of memory, any error from the
module. To turn it off: untick Hardware acceleration on the window menu,
or *Set Reel$NoOverlay 1 (or *Set EGL$Overlay off, which turns overlays
off for every program) before starting Reel.

Subtitles
---------
Reel shows subtitles on the picture: white text with a black outline,
centred near the bottom, sized to the picture (so they grow with the
window and full screen), or the pictures of DVD, Blu-ray and DVB
subtitles where they belong. Like the stats, they're drawn into the
picture itself, so they show with hardware acceleration too.
  - A file's own subtitles (MKV, MP4 and others: SubRip, ASS/SSA, MP4
    text, WebVTT, DVD, Blu-ray PGS, DVB): a track the file marks as the
    default (or forced) is shown at the start, as mpv does; others are
    on the Subtitles menu, or J.
  - Subtitle files: a file with the video's name and srt, ass, ssa or vtt
    after the "/" (film/mkv: film/srt) is found and shown when the video
    opens. Or drop a subtitle file on the window while the video plays.
  - V hides them and shows them again.
ASS/SSA styling (fonts, colours, positions, effects) isn't followed:
their text is shown plainly, one line under another. Characters outside
Latin-1 show as "?", except curly quotes, dashes and "..." which are
shown plainly.

Chapters, frame steps, turned videos
------------------------------------
Files with chapters (MKV, MP4, many films) list them on the Chapters
menu; Page Down and Page Up go to the next and back. While paused, "."
shows the next picture and "," the one before (going back decodes from
the key frame before, so it can take a moment); Play carries on from
the picture shown. Videos from phones that are stored sideways, with a
note in the file saying which way up they go, are shown upright (Media
info: Turned).

Stats on the picture
--------------------
S (or Stats on the picture on the window menu) draws a panel of figures
on the picture's top left corner, as YouTube's "Stats for nerds" does:
  Video / Source      the file, and its container
  Viewport / Frames   the picture area's size in pixels; pictures skipped
                      as late, of all pictures, since the start
  Current Res / Drawn the video's size and frame rate; how it's drawn
                      (hardware overlay, or converted)
  Volume              and the speed, if not normal
  Codecs, Color       the video's and sound's codecs; its colours
  For a web address:
  Connection Speed    how fast it's being read, with the last minute as
                      a graph
  Network Activity    how much was read in the last second, and a graph
  Buffer Health       how much is read ahead, and a graph
  For a file, instead:
  Reading             how fast the file is being read
  Ready               pictures decoded ahead, and the sound queued
  Timing              decoding and drawing each picture, how busy
                      decoding is, and how far the picture is from the
                      sound
  Keeping Up          only when decoding is too slow: what's being left
                      out (deblocking, frames)
  Date                today's date and the time
It's drawn into the picture itself, not in a window over it, so it can be
seen over a hardware overlay too (which covers anything else on the
screen), and moving it doesn't hide the overlay. Screen grabs don't see it
through an overlay. The figures change once a second. Media info has more.

Keeping time
------------
The sound is what Reel keeps time by: the sound hardware plays at its own
rate, and the pictures have to keep to it. But the sound system only says
how far it has got every 46 ms or so (a block at a time), less often than
the pictures come (every 33 ms at 30 fps, 17 ms at 60). So Reel runs a
clock of its own from the computer's timer, which is smooth, and steers
it gently towards the sound each time the sound reports, so the two never
drift apart. Media info shows both: how evenly the pictures come, and how
closely the clock follows the sound.

Carrying on where you stopped
-----------------------------
Reel remembers where you stopped each file (the last 100). Open one
again and it asks whether to carry on from there or start again. Files
played to the end, or hardly started, aren't remembered.

Choices
-------
The volume, Deinterlace, Keep on top, Hardware acceleration and where the mini player goes are saved in
<Choices$Write>.Reel.Choices (ReelEGL for !ReelEGL); where files were
stopped in <Choices$Write>.Reel.Resume. The other options are for this
session only.

NEON
----
Besides FFmpeg's own NEON code (H.264, VP8/VP9, AV1 through dav1d,
MPEG-1/2/4, HEVC's brightness), this port adds NEON for:
  - deinterlacing (yadif);
  - HEVC's colour (chroma motion compensation);
  - resizing and colour conversion to the screen whenever the picture
    isn't shown at its own size: full screen, a resized window, the
    mini player, Fill and Stretch.
Each gives exactly the same pictures as FFmpeg's plain C code.

Media info
----------
A window (it stays open while you watch; I or the window menu) with,
at the top, "stats for nerds", updated every second while playing:

  Position, Clock   where the picture is, and the sound's clock, with
                    how far apart they are (the sound only reports its
                    progress every 46 ms or so, so this wobbles); then
                    how closely Reel's own clock follows the sound: the
                    lip sync
  Pictures shown    a second, against the video's own frame rate; how
                    many were skipped as late; and how evenly they came
                    (the average error in the time between two pictures)
  Decoded           pictures decoded a second, the time each took, and
                    how many times faster than real time that is
  Decoding load     the share of the time spent decoding
  Frame skipping    whether Reel is skipping frames to keep up, and
                    whether it has turned deblocking off to keep up
  Converting        swscale's time a picture (colours and scaling)
  Deinterlacing     Auto/On/Off, the time a picture, and how many
                    pictures were interlaced
  Drawing           the time to put each picture on the screen
  Waiting           pictures decoded and packets read but not yet used
  Sound             the sound output and how much is queued
  Reading           how fast the file is being read; for a web address,
                    how much is read ahead
  Desktop           null events a second, how much of the time Reel
                    was asleep, and the screen mode

and below that what's in the file: the container, length, size and bit
rate; the video's codec, profile, level, size, aspect, frame rate, pixel
format and colours; the sound's codec and profile (e.g. HE-AAC),
channels, sample rate and bit rate; and the sound output used.
Times are measured with the centisecond clock, so they're averages.

Log
---
Reel writes a log as it goes, started afresh each time Reel starts:
<Wimp$ScrapDir>.ReelLog (ReelEGLLog for !ReelEGL). Log on the icon's
menu opens it. It has the sound modules found (and their versions), the
screen mode, each file opened and what's in it, what you did (pause,
seek, full screen), any errors, FFmpeg's messages, and once a second
while playing: the position, the clock, the sound (queued, and
StreamManager's added and played counts) and how many pictures were
shown, and what each picture cost in milliseconds: decoding,
converting (to what size), drawing and deinterlacing - in full screen
too, where Media info can't be seen. If something goes wrong, the log
says what Reel saw.
To put it somewhere else: *Set Reel$Log <file> before starting Reel;
*Set Reel$Log off for none.

Speed
-----
Everything is decoded on the one CPU core. On a Raspberry Pi 4, 720p
H.264 plays well at normal bitrates (up to about 5 Mbit/s); very high
bitrate files (like 25 Mbit/s camera or "HD" downloads) fall behind.
Then Reel keeps up with the sound: 0.3 s behind, it stops decoding the
frames nothing else needs (most B-frames); 1.5 s behind, it decodes only
keyframes until it has caught up. The log says when. To make a file play
smoothly, convert it once, e.g. on a faster computer:

  ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -c:v libx264 -preset slow -tune fastdecode -b:v 3M
         -vf scale=-2:720 -c:a aac -b:a 128k out.mp4

A smaller window is cheaper than full screen: the picture is scaled to
the window.

Between pictures Reel sleeps (Wimp_PollIdle) until the next one is due,
having decoded the next few already, so other programs get the time and
Task Manager shows Reel using about what decoding and drawing need.
*Set Reel$NoSleep 1 (ReelEGL$NoSleep) before starting it polls flat out
instead, as before 0.1.9, to compare.

Needs
-----
Files over 2GB (up to 4GB-1, the largest RISC OS allows) play: Reel is
built with UnixLib 5.0.2.
Included: PThreadTicker 0.01, a small module from UnixLib (the C
library Reel is built with) for its thread timer; !Run loads it (a copy
merged into !System first). Reel plays files on one thread; a web
address is read by a second one, and SDL's sound starts one too.
Licence: docs.Licences.PThreadTicker (BSD).
SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (PackMan). For https
addresses, AcornSSL (part of RISC OS 5.28 and later). For sound:
SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager, John Duffell's modules (SharedSound,
which they use, is part of RISC OS). Download ssb.zip from Andrew
Sellors' RDPClient page:
  https://orac.co.uk/software/rdpclient/rdpclient.html
and merge its !System into yours; Reel's !Run loads them. John Duffell's
own site (now on the Internet Archive) has more details:
  https://web.archive.org/web/20110920080106/http://www.duffell.riscos.me.uk/
Without them Reel plays the picture, keeps time itself, and Media info
says why there is no sound.
Reel gives the sound straight to SharedSoundBuffer (from 0.1.5; before,
it went through SDL). If the stream opens but doesn't play (for about a
second nothing it was given is used up), Reel carries on without sound;
Media info then says "the device isn't playing". To try SDL's sound
instead: *Set REELCORE_AUDIO sdl before starting Reel.

!ReelEGL
--------
The zip also has !ReelEGL: the same player, drawing through riscos-mesa's
EGL (an example program for EGL; full screen can write straight into
screen memory). See its !Help.

Known limits
------------
- One video at a time (a playlist plays them in turn). No subtitles yet.
- Files with no video (sound only) aren't played.
- Web addresses: not DASH (.mpd) manifests; the Sound track menu is for
  files only. Reel doesn't run yt-dlp itself.

